Ladybird now moves a query to a new UDP connection to the configured
DNS server when it’s due for a retry and nothing has come back on its
connection since it went out, and keeps the old connection taking
answers for five seconds more. Chrome’s DNS client does much the
same, per attempt: Its DnsTransactionImpl::MakeUdpAttempt() asks for a
new, randomly bound socket for each attempt, and keeps the earlier
attempts in its attempts_ list, so a late answer to one of them still
completes the transaction. Chrome also aborts in-flight lookups and
clears its caches in HostResolverManager::OnIPAddressChanged(), which
Ladybird has no counterpart to. Gecko sends no DNS over UDP itself; its
plain lookups go through the system resolver, whose getaddrinfo()
opens a socket for each lookup. Those are from reading the sources.
The failure was checked in a Linux network namespace: A UDP socket
connected to 1.1.1.1 kept its Wi-Fi source address and kept sending
without error after the default route moved to another interface,
while a new socket took that interface’s address.
